# Journal

########## Week 1 – Verilog Basics ##########

### Goal

Learn the fundamentals of Verilog and become comfortable writing simple combinational logic modules.

### Completed Projects

-  AND Gate
-  OR Gate
-  XOR Gate
-  2:1 Multiplexer
-  1:2 Demultiplexer
-  2-to-4 Decoder

### What I Learned

During this week I learned the basic structure of a Verilog module and how hardware description languages differ from traditional programming languages.

I became familiar with:

- "module"
- "input" and "output"
- "wire"
- "assign"
- combinational logic
- truth tables
- writing testbenches
- verifying designs using simulation

I also learned how common digital building blocks such as multiplexers, demultiplexers and decoders are used to route and select data in digital systems.

### Challenges

At the beginning it took some time to adjust to thinking in terms of hardware rather than sequential software execution.

Another challenge was understanding the purpose of the different logic components instead of only memorizing their truth tables.

### How I Solved Them

I used waveform simulations together with truth tables to verify every design.

Reading the documentation and comparing the behavior of each circuit helped me understand why each component exists and where it is used.

### Reflection

This week gave me a solid introduction to digital design.

I now feel comfortable writing simple combinational circuits in Verilog and verifying them using simulations.

Although the individual projects were relatively small, they introduced many of the concepts that will be reused throughout the rest of this learning journey.

### Next Step

Sequential logic.

I will learn how clocks, flip-flops, registers and finite state machines work before moving on to larger computer components.


########## Week 2 – Sequential Logic ##########

### Goal

Learn how sequential logic works and understand how digital circuits can store information and change their state over time.

### Completed Projects

- D Flip-Flop
- 8-bit Register
- 8-bit Shift Register
- 8-bit Counter
- Traffic Light FSM
- Elevator FSM
- UART State Machine

### What I Learned

During this stage I learned how sequential logic differs from combinational logic.

I learned how a clock signal can be used to synchronize changes in a digital circuit and how flip-flops can store information.

The main concepts I worked with were:

- Clock signals
- Rising edges (`posedge`)
- Reset
- D Flip-Flops
- Registers
- Counters
- Shift Registers
- Finite State Machines (FSMs)
- State transitions
- State encoding
- Serial communication
- UART

I also learned how several smaller components can work together. For example, an FSM can control what operation should happen while a counter keeps track of timing or position.

### Challenges

The biggest conceptual challenge was understanding the difference between combinational and sequential logic.

With combinational logic, the output depends directly on the current inputs. With sequential logic, the circuit can store information and its behavior depends on its previous state.

FSMs were another important challenge. It was necessary to think about the system as a collection of states and transitions instead of just individual logic operations.

### How I Solved Them

I used simulations and waveforms to observe how signals changed over time.

For the FSM projects, I found it useful to find a diagram and a state table before writing the Verilog implementation.

Breaking larger problems into smaller components also made the projects easier to understand.

### Reflection

This stage was a significant step forward from the first stage.

In Verilog Basics, I mainly learned how to describe simple combinational logic. During Sequential Logic, I learned how hardware can store information, keep track of its state and react to events over time.

The FSM projects were especially useful because they showed how the concepts from the earlier projects can be combined to create more complex control systems.

The UART state machine also gave me my first introduction to how digital logic can be used to control a real communication protocol.

I now have a much better understanding of the basic building blocks used in larger digital systems.

### What I Can Do Now

After completing this stage, I can:

- Write basic sequential Verilog
- Use clocks and resets
- Create registers and counters
- Shift data between registers
- Design simple FSMs
- Create state diagrams and state tables
- Understand basic UART transmission logic
- Verify sequential designs using simulation and waveforms

### Next Step

The next stage will focus on building larger computer components.

I will start by implementing a UART from scratch, followed by SPI, memory components and an ALU.

The goal is to move from individual digital building blocks toward the components that make up a computer system.


########## Journal – Step 3: Computer Components ##########

## What I Did

During this step, I focused on building and understanding several of the hardware components that are needed to construct a computer system.

I started by building a UART from scratch in Verilog. I implemented a Baud Rate Generator, UART Transmitter and UART Receiver, and verified their behavior using simulation.

After UART, I built an SPI communication system consisting of an SPI Master and SPI Slave. I simulated the communication between them to verify that data was transferred correctly.

I then moved on to memory components. I implemented a ROM, followed by RAM, and finally a Register File. These projects helped me understand how data can be stored, addressed, read and written in digital hardware.

The final project of this step was a Simple ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit). I implemented several arithmetic and logical operations, including addition, subtraction, AND, OR, XOR and shift operations.

## What I Learned

The most important thing I learned during this step is how individual digital components can be combined to form more complex computer hardware.

I learned about:

- UART
- SPI
- Serial communication
- Baud rate and timing
- Master/Slave communication
- ROM
- RAM
- Memory addressing
- Register Files
- Read and write operations
- ALUs
- Combinational logic
- CPU datapaths

I also became more comfortable with Verilog, testbenches and waveform analysis using GTKWave.

## Challenges

One of the more difficult parts was understanding the timing involved in communication protocols.

With UART and SPI, data has to be transmitted and sampled at the correct time. Using GTKWave made it much easier to understand what was actually happening to the signals during simulation.

Memory components were also challenging because I had to think more about how data is stored and accessed rather than simply performing logical operations.

The Register File was particularly useful because it introduced the idea of multiple registers being accessed by a CPU.

## Problems I Solved

Most of the problems I encountered were solved through simulation and waveform analysis.

When a signal did not behave as expected, I could inspect it in GTKWave and determine where the problem occurred.

This taught me that debugging hardware is not only about looking at the Verilog code. It is also important to analyze the actual behavior of the simulated circuit.

## Reflection

This step felt much more connected to building a real computer than the previous steps.

Earlier, I was mainly building individual components such as AND gates, multiplexers, registers and counters. During this step, I started building components that are actually used as parts of larger computer systems.

One of the most important things I realized is that a CPU is not one extremely complicated component. It is made up of many smaller components that work together.

For example, a Register File can provide two operands to an ALU, the ALU performs an operation on those operands, and the result can then be written back into a register.

This gave me a much clearer understanding of how a CPU datapath works.

## What I Would Improve

If I were to redo some of the projects, I would spend more time testing edge cases and creating more extensive testbenches.

I would also like to experiment with the components on a real FPGA board once I have one available, rather than only verifying them through simulation.

## Next Step

Step 3 is now complete.

The next step is Step 4 – Understanding SoC Architecture.

Instead of focusing on individual components, I will now study how they are connected together in a complete system.

The architecture I will focus on is:

CPU

↓

Memory Bus

↓

Memory  
UART  
GPIO  
Timer

↓

Interrupts

The goal is to understand how these components communicate with each other and how a complete SoC is structured before moving further towards RISC-V and Linux.

